  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/8bfb5e934d6ce86fc478c5ff200db616f65c95956030ccfb247e526551ee8a37.png)\n\n  \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{3}),$ $(-\\sqrt{3},2),$ and $(2,6).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-\\sqrt{3},2].$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,\\sqrt{3}),$ $(-\\sqrt{3},2],$ and $(2,6).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[-1,2],$ because $[-1,2]$ is contained inside the interval $(-\\sqrt{3},2].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $q(x)$ is not left-continuous at $x=2,$ so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at some point in the interval $(0,2).$  So it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$",
